import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var a={seasons:{theory:{concept:"[[The seasons are the yearly changes of temperature and day length caused by the tilt of the Earth's axis \u2014 about 23.4\xB0 from the perpendicular to its orbital plane \u2014 not by changes in the Earth's distance from the Sun.]] The axis keeps pointing the same way in space as the Earth goes round the Sun, so for half of the year each hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun and for the other half away from it. When a hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun, the Sun climbs higher, its rays fall more steeply and heat each square metre more, and the days are longer: it is summer there. The seasons in the two hemispheres are opposite; in fact, the Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, during the northern winter."},section:{myth:{title:"It is not about distance",text:"Many people think summer comes when the Earth is closest to the Sun. It is not so: the Earth is closest on about 3 January (147 million km) and farthest on about 4 July (152 million km). Northern winter happens when we are nearest to the Sun! If distance made the seasons, the whole Earth would have summer at the same time \u2014 but when it is summer in Georgia, it is winter in Argentina."},tilt:{title:"The tilted axis",text:"[[The Earth's axis is tilted by \u03B5 = 23.44\xB0 from the perpendicular to its orbit, and during the whole year it points towards the same spot in the sky (near the Pole Star).]] In June the northern end leans towards the Sun, in December it leans away. At the equinoxes in March and September the axis leans sideways \u2014 neither towards nor away from the Sun \u2014 and the Sun is above the equator. Set the tilt to 0\xB0 and the seasons disappear completely."},height:{title:"How high is the Sun?",text:"At noon the Sun stands highest. Its height is h = 90\xB0 \u2212 |\u03C6 \u2212 \u03B4|, where \u03C6 is the latitude and \u03B4 is the declination \u2014 the latitude where the Sun is straight overhead that day. \u03B4 swings between +\u03B5 in June and \u2212\u03B5 in December. In Tbilisi (\u03C6 = 41.7\xB0 N) the noon Sun reaches 71.7\xB0 in June but only 24.9\xB0 in December. A low Sun spreads the same light over a bigger patch of ground and sends it through more air, so it warms the ground much less."},length:{title:"How long is the day?",text:"A higher Sun also means a longer day. When the Sun is north of the equator (\u03B4 > 0), the northern hemisphere has more than half of each turning circle in sunlight \u2014 and long days. In Tbilisi the day lasts about 15 hours in June and 9 hours in December; at the equinoxes it is exactly 12 hours everywhere on Earth. Long days and a high Sun together make the summer warm."},orbit:{title:"The shape of the orbit",text:"The orbit is a very slightly stretched ellipse (e = 0.0167). [[By Kepler's second law the Earth moves faster near the Sun, so northern winter is a little shorter than northern summer: the half-year from the March to the September equinox lasts about 186 days, the other half about 179.]] Try a stretched orbit (e = 0.3 or 0.6) to see how distance can start to matter \u2014 but on the real Earth the tilt wins by far."},poles:{title:"The poles and the equator",text:"Beyond the polar circles (above 66.56\xB0, which is 90\xB0 \u2212 \u03B5) the Sun does not set around the June solstice in the north \u2014 the polar day \u2014 and does not rise around the December solstice \u2014 the polar night. At the poles the year is one long day and one long night. The equator has 12-hour days all year, and the Sun passes overhead twice a year, so it has hardly any seasons at all."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"Where and when is the noon Sun straight overhead? (At \u03C6 = \u03B4: on the equator at the equinoxes, on the Tropic of Cancer, 23.44\xB0 N, on 21 June.) What happens on a planet with \u03B5 = 90\xB0? (The Sun goes round and round overhead at a pole and the seasons are extreme.) Is the Earth's distance to the Sun to blame for winter? (No \u2014 try 'closest in winter'.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{declination:"Declination: where the Sun is overhead; \u03BB is the Sun's position along the year (0\xB0 in March, 90\xB0 in June)",altitude:"Height of the noon Sun above the horizon at latitude \u03C6",hourAngle:"Half of the daylight arc: H\u2080 is the Sun's angle from south at sunrise",dayLength:"Length of daylight",intensity:"Intensity of noon sunlight on level ground (S\u2080: solar constant)",insolation:"Sunlight per day, averaged over 24 hours (above the atmosphere)"},variable:{tilt:"Tilt of the Earth's axis",latitude:"Latitude of the observer",declination:"Declination of the Sun",altitude:"Height of the noon Sun",distance:"Distance from the Sun",solarConstant:"Solar constant (sunlight at 1 AU)"},assumption:{point:"The Sun is treated as a point, so day length is exact only to a few minutes (the real day is slightly longer).",air:"Sunlight is measured above the atmosphere; air, clouds and the oceans \u2014 which make real summers lag behind the Sun \u2014 are ignored.",steady:"The orbit and the tilt never change, and dates are for a common year without leap days.",scale:"Not to scale: the Sun and the Earth are drawn far larger than their distance allows (the real Earth is about 23 000 times smaller than its orbit)."},param:{tilt:"Tilt of the axis",latitude:"Latitude of the observer",startDay:"Start date (day of the year)",daysPerSecond:"Speed of time",eccentricity:"Stretching of the orbit",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{tilt:"The tilt makes the seasons. Try 0\xB0 and 90\xB0.",latitude:"Positive is north of the equator, negative is south.",startDay:"0 is 1 January, 78 is the March equinox. The run starts from this date.",eccentricity:"0 is a perfect circle. The Earth's orbit is almost one."},preset:{noTilt:"No tilt",earth:"Earth",mars:"Mars",strong:"Strong",sideways:"Lying on its side",equator:"Equator",tbilisi:"Tbilisi",arctic:"Arctic",northPole:"North Pole",buenosAires:"Buenos Aires",marchEquinox:"March equinox",juneSolstice:"June solstice",septemberEquinox:"September equinox",decemberSolstice:"December solstice",circle:"Circle",stretched:"Stretched",veryStretched:"Very stretched"},measure:{noonAltitude:"Height of the noon Sun",dayLength:"Length of daylight",nightLength:"Length of night",declination:"Declination of the Sun",insolation:"Sunlight per day, averaged",distance:"Distance from the Sun",time:"Time since the start",noonIntensity:"Intensity of noon sunlight",distanceKm:"Distance from the Sun",orbitalSpeed:"Orbital speed",altitudeSwing:"Yearly swing of the noon Sun",summerHalf:"Sun north of the equator (March \u2192 September)",winterHalf:"Sun south of the equator (September \u2192 March)"},month:{"1":"January","2":"February","3":"March","4":"April","5":"May","6":"June","7":"July","8":"August","9":"September","10":"October","11":"November","12":"December"},view:{observer:"{latitude} \xB7 noon Sun {altitude}",noSunrise:"{latitude} \xB7 the Sun does not rise",day:"Day {day} \xB7 night {night}",north:"North",sun:"Sun",status:"{date} \xB7 {hemisphere}: {season}",date:"{date} {month}",northHemisphere:"Northern hemisphere",southHemisphere:"Southern hemisphere",noSeason:"no seasons",season:{spring:"spring",summer:"summer",autumn:"autumn",winter:"winter"},legend:"Bright ring: the observer's day \xB7 dark ring: night",marchEquinox:"Equinox {date}",juneSolstice:"Solstice {date}",septemberEquinox:"Equinox {date}",decemberSolstice:"Solstice {date}",perihelion:"Closest",aphelion:"Farthest",followOption:"Follow the Earth",latitudeOption:"Day and night along the latitude",raysOption:"Noon ray and horizon",markersOption:"Equinoxes and solstices",apsidesOption:"Closest and farthest points"},graph:{daylight:"Day and night length through the year",altitude:"Noon Sun and declination through the year",insolation:"Sunlight through the year"},challenge:{midnightSun:{title:"The midnight Sun",description:"Make the Sun stay up for all 24 hours.",hint:"Choose the June solstice and a place far in the north, for example the Arctic."},polarNight:{title:"The polar night",description:"Make a place where the Sun does not rise at all.",hint:"Choose the December solstice and the Arctic or the North Pole."},overhead:{title:"The Sun straight overhead",description:"Make the noon Sun stand exactly overhead at your place.",hint:"The Sun is overhead where the latitude equals its declination: try the equator at an equinox, or 23.4\xB0 on a solstice."},noSeasons:{title:"A planet without seasons",description:"Remove the seasons for an observer in the middle latitudes.",hint:"Without a tilt the Sun stays above the equator all year."},closestWinter:{title:"Closest to the Sun \u2014 in winter",description:"Find a moment when the Earth is close to the Sun (below 0.985 AU) and it is winter in the northern hemisphere.",hint:"Start on day 2 (3 January)."},tiltedSun:{title:"The midnight Sun in the middle latitudes",description:"Make the Sun stay up all day at a latitude of 50\xB0 or less.",hint:"The polar circle lies at 90\xB0 \u2212 tilt, so you need a tilt of at least 40\xB0."}}}},n={sim:a};export{n as default,a as sim};
